    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p11-q21-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "What copy size would you create by pushing button A then B then C?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$54\\%$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Copy size}&=0.75\\times0.80\\times0.90\\times100\\%\\\\\\text{Copy size}&=54\\%\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q21-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "What two buttons can you press to achieve a copy size of $60\\%$?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "A and B, in either order.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Copy size using A and B}&=0.75\\times0.80\\times100\\%\\\\\\text{Copy size using A and B}&=60\\%\\end{align*}$$\n\nPress A and B in either order.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q21-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "What three buttons can you press to achieve a copy size of $180\\%$?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "B, D and D, in any order.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Copy size using B, D and D}&=0.80\\times1.50\\times1.50\\times100\\%\\\\\\text{Copy size using B, D and D}&=180\\%\\end{align*}$$\n\nPress B once and D twice, in any order.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q21-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "What three buttons can you press to achieve a copy size of $96\\%$?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "B, B and D, in any order.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Copy size using B, B and D}&=0.80\\times0.80\\times1.50\\times100\\%\\\\\\text{Copy size using B, B and D}&=96\\%\\end{align*}$$\n\nPress B twice and D once, in any order.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q21-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "Explain why button C is unnecessary.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "A, B and D together produce $90\\%$, so they can replace C.\n\nMultiply their copy-size multipliers.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Copy size using A, B and D}&=0.75\\times0.80\\times1.50\\times100\\%\\\\\\text{Copy size using A, B and D}&=90\\%\\end{align*}$$\n\nEach press of C can be replaced by pressing A, B and D.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q21-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "Is it possible to achieve a copy size of $65\\%$? Give reasons.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No.\n\nThe button multipliers contain only prime factors $2$, $3$ and $5$, whereas $65\\%=\\frac{13}{20}$ requires a factor of $13$.",
          "worked": "Button C can be replaced by A, B and D. The remaining multipliers are\n\n$$75\\%=\\frac34,\\qquad80\\%=\\frac45,\\qquad150\\%=\\frac32.$$\n\nAny product of these fractions has only $2$, $3$ and $5$ as prime factors in its numerator and denominator, even after cancellation. However,\n\n$$65\\%=\\frac{13}{20}.$$\n\nIts numerator contains the prime factor $13$, so no sequence of button presses can produce $65\\%$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 62
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q21-g",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "g",
        "prompt": "Does the order in which the buttons are pressed matter?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No; multiplication is commutative.",
          "worked": "Each button multiplies the current copy size by its multiplier. Changing the order of the same factors leaves their product unchanged, so the order does not matter.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      }
    ]
